WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.920
What makes predictive modeling difficult? We have&nbsp;
millions of patients we want to analyze their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:18.560
diagnosis information, medication information, and&nbsp;
so on. All this data combined together, create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.480
big challenge. The second challenge in predictive&nbsp;
modeling is, there's so many models to be built.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.560
Predictive modeling is not a single algorithm,&nbsp;
it's a sequence of computational tasks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.920
We've introduced predictive modeling pipeline&nbsp;
in more details in the previous lecture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.640
The truth is, every step in this&nbsp;
pipeline has many different options.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.520
All of those combined give us many&nbsp;
pipelines to be evaluated and compared.&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.560
You might be wondering why do we need different&nbsp;
algorithms for one problem. Are five different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.080
algorithms really necessary? Can't we just&nbsp;
decide which algorithm works best and use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.440
that for every problem? We did look at when you&nbsp;
should and shouldn't use each of these algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.000
whether there is limited data, performance&nbsp;
thresholds, or compute power limitations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.480
This isn't one size fits all. There's no&nbsp;
free lunch theorem works for every problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.400
We know some of the tendencies&nbsp;
and strengths of each algorithm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.360
We can use that to narrow down the algorithms&nbsp;
that we're considering for a given problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.520
But what are the questions that we need to be&nbsp;
asking? When do we not to use certain algorithms?&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.000
&nbsp;
Generally, these questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:39.680
fall under two broad categories. Accuracy and&nbsp;
latency. We'll start with latency. We talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:46.080
about how logistic regression is really fast&nbsp;
to train, while SVM are quite slow. So, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.960
have limited compute power or limited time, then&nbsp;
you'll want to lean toward logistic regression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:58.000
The next question is, how long? Maybe, if you have&nbsp;
a lot of real time data coming in that you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.200
make really fast, real time predictions on, then&nbsp;
you might want to consider logistic regression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:10.320
Now, moving over to accuracy. What does&nbsp;
our data look like? Is it short and fat?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.800
Is it long and skinny? For instance, if&nbsp;
it's short and fat, in other words, it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.840
a lot of features but very few rows, we talked&nbsp;
about how SVM does quite well in this space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.320
The next question is just how complex is this&nbsp;
dataset? Are there clear connections that should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.320
be easy to pick up on? If so, then we should&nbsp;
start thinking about prioritizing time to train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:40.400
or predict. However, if the relationships are&nbsp;
really complex, then we'll probably start thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.760
about something like multilayer perceptrons, or&nbsp;
SVM, where they take a little longer to train,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.160
but they're much better at picking up&nbsp;
on these really complex relationships.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:57.200
Lastly, how messy is our data? If there are a lot&nbsp;
of outliers, then SVM is probably a good choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:03.280
while logistic regression probably is not. Using&nbsp;
the tendencies and strengths of the algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:08.880
we can narrow down the algorithms considered&nbsp;
for a given problem. But, the bottom line is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:14.320
sometimes we just don't know which algorithm&nbsp;
will perform best. So, we just need to test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.680
a few and see which actually does perform&nbsp;
best on the data that we have. Generally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.800
you'll start with maybe six or seven algorithms to&nbsp;
consider, you'll narrow it down to three or four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.360
with these questions, and then you'll just test&nbsp;
those three or four with various hyper parameter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.520
settings to try to find the best model for the&nbsp;
data and problem that you have in front of you.&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.840
We've learned about some of the&nbsp;
strengths and weaknesses of each.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:44.400
Let’s compare these 5 algorithms across 6 key&nbsp;
dimensions, before we run an actual comparison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:50.480
of each model. The 6 dimensions that will compare&nbsp;
across these algorithms are problem type, time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.840
train, time to make predictions, interpretability,&nbsp;
performance and performance with limited data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.600
This table will help you to&nbsp;
decide when to use each of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.280
algorithms.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.160
For problem type, logistic regression and&nbsp;
SVM can only be used in classification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.680
In other words, they can only be used for&nbsp;
problems with a categorical target variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.480
Multilayer perceptron, random forest, and boosted&nbsp;
trees can be used for both classification and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:24.640
regression. In other words, they can be used for&nbsp;
both categorical and continuous target variables.&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.040
&nbsp;
Training speed. This is particularly important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.880
If you have a lot of data or&nbsp;
you have compute restrictions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:41.280
In those cases, training speed can be prohibited.&nbsp;
Random forest is relatively fast. And the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.520
three are really quite slow, with SVM probably&nbsp;
being the slowest. This also will depend on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.880
hyperparameter settings for each algorithm.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.840
For prediction speed, boosted trees and&nbsp;
logistic regression is considered quite fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.240
Again, recall that boosted trees&nbsp;
are trained in a parallel manner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:06.240
so it's quite fast. The other 3 algorthms,&nbsp;
although quite fast, but is behind logistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.800
regression and boosted trees.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.760
For interpretability, the reality is that none&nbsp;
of these models are particularly interpretable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.520
Logistic regression is probably the most&nbsp;
interpretable by offering some tools to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:25.600
what's going on under the hood. Of the other&nbsp;
4 algorithms, boosted trees and random forest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.400
is slightly better than multilayer perceptron&nbsp;
and SVM, because at least they offer that how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.520
different features are contributing to your model.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:39.280
Performance is a big one for obvious reasons.&nbsp;
Boosted trees is slow to train and it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.080
interpretable, but it's highly flexible and&nbsp;
typically performs very well on almost any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:50.080
problem. Multilayer perceptron also perform&nbsp;
well on most problems, but it's a step behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:56.720
boosted trees. Random forest and SVM are then&nbsp;
a step behind those two and logistic regression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:02.320
comes in last on performance. Again, this is&nbsp;
subject to things like the size of the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:05.760
the shape of the data, and how messy your data is.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.720
Now in the big data era, it may seem odd to have&nbsp;
a column here for performance on limited data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.600
But, you might surprise that a lot of the&nbsp;
industries deal with small set of data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.160
but with a lot of features. SVM is&nbsp;
the one that really excels here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.960
Especially when you have a lot of features, but&nbsp;
not a lot of examples. Logistic regression also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:32.880
does quite well with limited data. The one that&nbsp;
is severely disadvantaged with limited data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:37.840
is multilayer perceptron. Now that&nbsp;
we've compared all these algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:47.840
let’s compare them all based on performance&nbsp;
on the validation set in the next lecture.

